new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6db14dd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,111 @@
+## Deployment
+
+Fizzy can run on a single Linux server with Docker. Everything lives in one
+container: the Rails app, background jobs (Solid Queue runs inside Puma),
+SQLite databases, and uploaded files. [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) builds
+and deploys the container, and [kamal-proxy](https://github.com/basecamp/kamal-proxy)
+provides automatic HTTPS through Let's Encrypt.
+
+### Requirements
+
+- A Linux server with SSH access, ideally 2 GB of RAM or more. Docker is
+  installed automatically by `kamal setup` if it isn't already present.
+- A hostname whose DNS `A`/`AAAA` record points at the server. Kamal connects
+  to the server over SSH using this hostname, so the DNS record must exist
+  before setup. Ports 80 and 443 must be open so kamal-proxy can obtain a
+  certificate and serve HTTPS.
+- Docker and an SSH key for the server on the machine you deploy from.
+
+### Setup
+
+1. Fork and clone the repository, then install the gems:
+
+   ```sh
+   bundle install
+   ```
+
+2. Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+   - Replace every occurrence of `fizzy.example.com` with your hostname (in
+     `servers.web`, `proxy.host` and `env.clear`). If you need to reach the
+     server over a different address than the public hostname, replace only
+     `servers.web` with that address.
+   - Replace the `SMTP_*` and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` values with the settings
+     for the mail server that should send Fizzy emails. If your mail server
+     doesn't require authentication, leave `SMTP_USERNAME` and
+     `SMTP_PASSWORD` blank.
+   - Change `builder.arch` to `arm64` if your server is ARM-based.
+   - If your mail server uses implicit TLS on port 465, set `SMTP_SSL: true` and
+     `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS: false` under `env.clear`.
+
+3. Fill in `.kamal/secrets`. It needs a `SECRET_KEY_BASE`, your SMTP password,
+   and a VAPID key pair for browser push notifications:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/rails secret
+   bundle exec ruby -rweb-push -e 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}"; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"'
+   ```
+
+   You can either export those variables in your shell before deploying, or
+   replace the `$VARIABLE` references in `.kamal/secrets` with the values
+   directly. If you write the values into the file, don't commit them: see the
+   comments at the top of `.kamal/secrets`.
+
+4. Deploy:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+   Kamal builds the image locally, pushes it to a local registry that is
+   tunneled to the server over SSH, installs Docker on the server if needed,
+   starts kamal-proxy, and boots the app. Your machine must stay connected for
+   the duration of the deploy.
+
+5. Visit `https://your-hostname` and sign up. Fizzy will email you a sign-in
+   code, so make sure the SMTP settings are correct before trying.
+
+### Data persistence
+
+The `fizzy_storage` Docker volume is mounted at `/rails/storage` and holds the
+SQLite databases and uploaded files. It is reused on every deploy, so data
+survives restarts and redeploys. New containers run migrations automatically
+on boot via `bin/docker-entrypoint`.
+
+Deploy future changes with:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+The container runs Solid Queue inside Puma (because `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA` is
+set to `true` in `config/deploy.yml`), so background jobs and recurring tasks
+run on the same box without a separate job container.
+
+### Backups
+
+Back up the volume regularly. For example, from your machine:
+
+```sh
+ssh root@your-server \
+  'docker run --rm -v fizzy_storage:/data -v /tmp:/backup alpine \
+     tar czf /backup/fizzy-storage.tgz -C /data .'
+scp root@your-server:/tmp/fizzy-storage.tgz .
+```
+
+For a more consistent SQLite backup, you can stop the app first with
+`bin/kamal app stop`, or use `sqlite3 ... ".backup ..."` inside the container.
+
+### Useful commands
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs              # Tail app logs
+bin/kamal console           # Rails console in the running container
+bin/kamal shell             # Shell in the running container
+bin/kamal details           # Container and proxy status
+bin/kamal redeploy          # Reboot the app without rebuilding
+bin/kamal rollback VERSION  # Roll back to a previous version
+```
+
+If the site isn't reachable, check that DNS resolves to the server, that ports
+80 and 443 are open, and inspect `bin/kamal logs` for boot errors.
